1
00:00:01,115 --> 00:00:08,917
Welcome to Europe episode six of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:09,566 --> 00:00:21,931
Our final nine-place group follows the Baltic countries south through Belarus
and Ukraine, then Moldova, Romania and Bulgaria, finishing with European
Russia.

3
00:00:22,599 --> 00:00:26,558
Let's bring Estonia and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:29,155 --> 00:00:40,579
Estonia is the northernmost Baltic country, directly south of Finland across
the Gulf of Finland. Latvia is below it, and Russia lies to its east.

5
00:00:41,243 --> 00:00:51,611
Estonian is closely related to Finnish. That language link points across the
gulf, even though Estonia’s location groups it with Latvia and Lithuania.

6
00:00:52,268 --> 00:01:01,602
Remember the Baltic stack from north to south: Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania.
Estonia is the one facing Finland across the water.

7
00:01:05,068 --> 00:01:16,922
Latvia is the middle Baltic country, below Estonia and above Lithuania. Its
coastline bends around the Gulf of Riga, with Russia and Belarus toward the
east.

8
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:29,870
Riga is renowned for its Art Nouveau buildings. Imagine decorative facades
beside a Baltic trading port: a visual hook for the middle country in our
stack.

9
00:01:30,529 --> 00:01:40,339
Keep Latvia between Estonia and Lithuania. Riga and the gulf share a name,
helping connect the cultural fact with the coastline.

10
00:01:42,898 --> 00:01:54,369
Lithuania is the southernmost Baltic country, below Latvia and above Poland
and Belarus. Russia’s separate Kaliningrad territory lies along its
southwestern side.

11
00:01:55,025 --> 00:02:08,632
On March eleventh, 1990, Lithuania declared the restoration of its
independence. That declaration became a defining moment in its departure from
Soviet rule.

12
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:20,173
Finish the Baltic stack: Estonia at the top, Latvia in the middle, Lithuania
at the bottom. Lithuania is the Baltic member nearest Poland.

13
00:02:20,867 --> 00:02:25,302
Let's bring Belarus and its neighbours into a closer view.

14
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:38,603
Belarus is inland, east of Poland, below Lithuania and Latvia, and north of
Ukraine. Russia lies along its eastern and northeastern side.

15
00:02:39,262 --> 00:02:53,043
The Bialowieza Forest crosses the Belarus-Poland border. It preserves old
forest habitat and supports European bison, linking this country to a living
landscape rather than a coastline.

16
00:02:53,698 --> 00:03:02,092
Remember Belarus above Ukraine and east of Poland. Picture the shared forest
and bison on its western edge.

17
00:03:04,716 --> 00:03:17,383
Ukraine lies south of Belarus and west of Russia, with Black Sea shores to the
south. Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania and Moldova connect it to central
and southeastern Europe.

18
00:03:18,045 --> 00:03:30,027
Kyiv was a major centre of medieval Kyivan Rus. Saint Sophia Cathedral and the
city’s historic monastic sites preserve connections with that long history.

19
00:03:30,681 --> 00:03:41,002
Keep Ukraine below Belarus and above the Black Sea. Kyiv is its historical
anchor; current occupation does not change Ukraine’s internationally
recognised borders.

20
00:03:41,706 --> 00:03:45,758
Let's bring Moldova and its neighbours into a closer view.

21
00:03:48,697 --> 00:03:58,124
Moldova is the narrow inland country between Romania and Ukraine. It comes
close to the Black Sea region but has no sea coast.

22
00:03:58,781 --> 00:04:12,167
Its Milestii Mici cellars occupy underground limestone galleries and hold a
wine collection recognised by Guinness World Records. Imagine long tunnels of
bottles under this small country.

23
00:04:12,825 --> 00:04:20,325
Remember Moldova squeezed between Romania and Ukraine: an inland sliver, with
underground wine cellars as its memory hook.

24
00:04:23,493 --> 00:04:33,512
Romania lies west of Moldova and Ukraine’s southwestern side, and north of
Bulgaria across the Danube. Its southeastern edge reaches the Black Sea.

25
00:04:34,176 --> 00:04:48,468
Bran Castle is marketed as Dracula’s Castle, but Bram Stoker’s fictional
castle and the historical ruler Vlad were different stories. A memorable
legend should not replace the geography or the history.

26
00:04:49,138 --> 00:04:59,842
Picture Romania above Bulgaria, with the Carpathian arc in its interior and a
Black Sea opening on the east. Keep Dracula as a story, not a location guide.

27
00:05:03,059 --> 00:05:14,576
Bulgaria lies directly below Romania, north of Greece and west of the Black
Sea. Serbia and North Macedonia are its western neighbours, with Türkiye to
the southeast.

28
00:05:15,235 --> 00:05:27,135
The Cyrillic alphabet developed in the First Bulgarian Empire around the ninth
and tenth centuries. Its later spread connects Bulgaria with many other
languages using Cyrillic today.

29
00:05:27,794 --> 00:05:39,520
Remember Bulgaria below Romania: the Danube helps divide the pair, and both
reach the Black Sea. The alphabet is Bulgaria’s cultural memory hook.

30
00:05:40,282 --> 00:05:44,078
Let's bring Russia and its neighbours into a closer view.

31
00:05:51,174 --> 00:06:04,433
Russia extends far beyond this European view. Here we see its western part,
east of Finland, the Baltic countries, Belarus and Ukraine; Kaliningrad is
separate on the Baltic.

32
00:06:05,092 --> 00:06:20,661
Its Lake Baikal is the world’s deepest lake, but lies in Siberia, in Asian
Russia, outside this map view. A country can span two continents without
becoming two countries.

33
00:06:21,319 --> 00:06:36,157
Remember European Russia continuing off the eastern edge, and keep Kaliningrad
separate from the main landmass. Our six-video route finishes here after
forty-five places, including the six supplemental microstates.

34
00:06:40,762 --> 00:06:56,076
The Baltic stack is Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania. Inland, Belarus is above
Ukraine; Moldova fits between Ukraine and Romania, while Bulgaria sits below
Romania along the Black Sea.

35
00:06:56,727 --> 00:07:11,703
Russia continues far beyond Europe; its Siberian facts belong outside this
European map view. Across six lessons we covered all thirty-nine GeoClick
Europe targets, plus six microstates in labelled supplemental views.

36
00:07:12,361 --> 00:07:31,153
This is the scope of our map-based series, not a universal definition of
Europe. Türkiye, Cyprus and the Caucasus are outside this map’s target list
and are left for neighbouring-region lessons. Rebuild the six groups using
their locations and memorable facts.
